I. Gilgamesh hersker i Uruk, og Enkidu bliver skabt
I. Gilgamesh rules Uruk, and Enkidu is made

Han, som prøvede alt til bunds, skal lære sit folk, hvad han ved. Han, som ved besked med alt, skal lære dem alle. Lad ham give sin visdom videre, så de kan dele den. [Gilgamesh?] kendte til alt. Han fandt den skjulte hemmelighed […] og gav os beretningen fra tiden før den store oversvømmelse. Han rejste langt. Træt og slidt af rejsen huggede han alt sit slid ind i en stentavle. Han byggede Uruks høje mure. Han fæstnede fundamentet under det hellige Eanna, hvor skatten lå, så det sad som støbt i bronze. Han styrkede dets grund, så ingen kunne trænge ind […] tærsklen, der fra gammel tid […].
Let the man who tested everything to its depths teach his people. Let him who knows it all instruct them all. Let him give them his wisdom to share. [Gilgamesh?] knew all things. He found the hidden secret […] and handed down the account from before the flood. He travelled far. Worn out by the journey, he carved all his labours on a stone tablet. He built the high walls of Uruk. He fastened the foundations of holy Eanna, where the treasure lay, firm as bronze. He strengthened its base so no one could pass […] the threshold which from ancient times […].

[Omkring tredive linjer mangler.] To tredjedele af ham er gud, en tredjedel menneske. Hans krops form […] han tvang […] [Omkring tre linjer mangler.]
[About thirty lines are missing.] Two thirds of him are divine, one third human. The shape of his body […] he forced […] [About three lines are missing.]

[…] Uruk har han taget […] Han knejser som en urokse. Ingen kan stå for hans våben. Hans mænd bliver drevet i fælden, Uruks krigere kryber sammen […]. Gilgamesh lader ingen far beholde sin søn. Dag og nat tager hans overmod til. Og han skal være hyrde for Uruk bag de høje mure! Han er vores hyrde […] hård, stærk, udspekuleret […]. Gilgamesh lader ingen mor beholde sin pige, ingen kriger sin brud, ingen mand sin kone.
[…] of Uruk he has taken […] He holds his head high like a wild bull. No one can withstand his weapons. His men are driven into the trap. Uruk's warriors cower […]. Gilgamesh leaves no father his son. Day and night his arrogance grows. Yet he is shepherd of high-walled Uruk! He is our shepherd […] forceful, commanding, cunning […]. Gilgamesh leaves no mother her girl, no warrior his bride, no husband his wife.

De udødelige hørte råbet. Himlens guder sagde til Anu, Uruks herre: »Du har fået en søn, der tyranniserer alle. Han knejser som en urokse. Ingen kan stå for hans våben. Hans mænd bliver drevet i fælden, Uruks krigere kryber sammen […]. Gilgamesh lader ingen far beholde sin søn. Dag og nat tager hans overmod til. Og han skal være hyrde for Uruk bag de høje mure! Han er deres hyrde […] hård, stærk, udspekuleret […]. Gilgamesh lader ingen mor beholde sin pige, ingen kriger sin brud, ingen mand sin kone.«
The immortals heard the cry. The gods of heaven spoke to Anu, lord of Uruk. “You have fathered a son who tyrannizes everyone. He holds his head high like a wild bull. No one can withstand his weapons. His men are driven into the trap. Uruk's warriors cower […]. Gilgamesh leaves no father his son. Day and night his arrogance grows. Yet he is shepherd of high-walled Uruk! He is their shepherd […] forceful, commanding, cunning […]. Gilgamesh leaves no mother her girl, no warrior his bride, no husband his wife.”

Anu hørte råbet. Han kaldte på gudinden Aruru: »Det var dig, Aruru, der skabte menneskenes første spire [?]. Skab nu én, der kan måle sig med ham. Lad ham møde Gilgamesh på en dag, hvor kongen søger sin fornøjelse. Lad de to prøve kræfter, så Uruk kan få fred.«
Anu heard the cry. He called the goddess Aruru. “Aruru, you made the first seed of mankind [?]. Make someone to match him now. Let him meet Gilgamesh on a day when the king seeks his pleasure. Let the two wrestle and give Uruk some peace.”

Da Aruru hørte det, tog Anus tanke form i hendes sind. Hun vaskede hænderne, tog en klump ler mellem fingrene og formede den ude på steppen. Dér skabte hun krigeren Enkidu, født som en anden Ninurta. Hele kroppen var dækket af hår. Håret på hovedet bandt han op som en kvinde. Det voksede tæt som stakkene på bygaks. Han kendte hverken folk eller land. Han gik klædt som kvægguden Sumuqan. Han græssede med gazellerne og drak sig utørstig sammen med kvæget. Med dyrene glædede han sig ved vandet.
When Aruru heard this, Anu's thought took shape in her mind. She washed her hands, took clay between her fingers and shaped it out on the steppe. There she made the warrior Enkidu, born like a second Ninurta. Hair covered his whole body. He tied up the hair on his head as a woman would. It grew as thick as the beards of barley. He knew neither people nor country. He dressed like Sumuqan, god of cattle. He grazed with the gazelles and drank his fill with the cattle. With the animals he delighted in the water.

En jæger, en mand der satte fælder, stod ansigt til ansigt med ham ved vandhullet. Én dag, to dage, tre dage så han ham dér. Ved synet blev jægeren bleg af skræk. Han gik hjem med sit kvæg, rystet og bange. Han jamrede sig. Hans hjerte var tungt, ansigtet mørkt […] sorgen sad i maven […]. Han så ud som en mand, der havde rejst langt.
A hunter who set traps came face to face with him at the water hole. One day, two days, three days he saw him there. The sight drained the colour from the hunter's face. He went home with his cattle, shaken and frightened. He cried aloud. His heart was heavy, his face dark […] grief deep in his belly […]. He looked like a man who had travelled far.

Jægeren sagde til sin far: »Far, der er kommet en stor mand ned fra bjergene. Ingen i hele landet er stærkere. Han er så stærk, at han kunne være Anus dobbeltgænger. Han strejfer om i bjergene, æder græs med dyrene og kommer gang på gang til vandet. Jeg tør ikke gå hen til ham. De huller, jeg selv har gravet, fylder han til igen. Han river mine fælder op og slipper steppens dyr ud af hænderne på mig. Jeg kan ikke passe min jagt for ham.«
The hunter said to his father, “Father, a great man has come down from the mountains. No one in all the land is stronger. He is so strong he might be Anu's double. He ranges over the mountains, grazes with the animals and keeps coming to the water. I'm afraid to go near him. He fills in the pits I dug myself. He tears up my traps and lets the animals of the steppe slip through my hands. He won't let me hunt.”

Hans far svarede: »I Uruk bor Gilgamesh, min søn. Ham har ingen besejret. Ingen i landet er stærkere. Han er så stærk, at han kunne være Anus dobbeltgænger. Gå til Uruk. Når han hører om sådan et væsen, vil han sige: Tag en skøge med dig, jæger. […] Når han igen samler dyrene ved vandet, skal hun lade sin kappe falde og vise ham sin skønhed. Han får øje på hende og tager hende i sin favn. Så vil de dyr, der voksede op på hans egen steppe, vende ham ryggen.«
His father answered, “My son, Gilgamesh lives in Uruk. No one has beaten him. No one in the land is stronger. He is so strong he might be Anu's double. Go to Uruk. When he hears of such a creature, he will say: Hunter, take a prostitute with you. […] When he gathers the animals at the water again, let her drop her cloak and show him her beauty. He will see her and take her in his arms. Then the animals raised on his own steppe will turn away from him.”

Jægeren lyttede til sin far og tog vejen til Uruk. Da han kom til Gilgamesh, sagde han: »Der er kommet en stor mand ned fra bjergene. Ingen i hele landet er stærkere. Han er så stærk, at han kunne være Anus dobbeltgænger. Han strejfer om i bjergene, æder græs med dyrene og kommer gang på gang til vandet. Jeg tør ikke gå hen til ham. De huller, jeg selv har gravet, fylder han til igen. Han river mine fælder op og slipper steppens dyr ud af hænderne på mig. Jeg kan ikke passe min jagt for ham.«
The hunter listened to his father and took the road to Uruk. Coming before Gilgamesh he said, “A great man has come down from the mountains. No one in all the land is stronger. He is so strong he might be Anu's double. He ranges over the mountains, grazes with the animals and keeps coming to the water. I'm afraid to go near him. He fills in the pits I dug myself. He tears up my traps and lets the animals of the steppe slip through my hands. He won't let me hunt.”

Gilgamesh svarede jægeren: »Gå, jæger, og tag en skøge med dig. Når han igen samler dyrene ved vandet, skal hun lade sin kappe falde og vise ham sin skønhed. Han får øje på hende og tager hende i sin favn. Så vil de dyr, der voksede op på hans egen steppe, vende ham ryggen.«
Gilgamesh answered the hunter. “Go, hunter, and take a prostitute with you. When he gathers the animals at the water again, let her drop her cloak and show him her beauty. He will see her and take her in his arms. Then the animals raised on his own steppe will turn away from him.”

Jægeren tog kvinden med, og de to drog af sted. Efter tre dage nåede de frem og satte sig til at vente. En dag, to dage sad de ved dyrenes vandhul. Så kom kvæget for at drikke sig utørstigt. Dyrene kom for at glæde sig ved vandet. Og der var Enkidu, ham bjergene havde født. Han græssede med gazellerne, drak sig utørstig med kvæget og glædede sig ved vandet sammen med dyrene. Kvinden så den store, stærke mand, et vildt væsen fra det inderste af steppen.
The hunter took the woman with him and the two set off. After three days they reached the place and settled down to wait. One day, two days they sat by the animals' water hole. Then the cattle came to drink their fill. The animals came to delight in the water. And there was Enkidu, the one the mountains had borne. He grazed with the gazelles, drank his fill with the cattle and delighted in the water with the animals. The woman saw the great, vigorous man, a wild creature from the heart of the steppe.

»Der er han! Vis ham din krop. Lad ham nyde din skønhed. Hold dig ikke tilbage. Tag pusten fra ham. Så snart han ser dig, kommer han hen til dig. Løsn din kappe, så han kan tage dig i sin favn. Vis ham, hvad en kvinde kan. Når han har holdt dig ind til sit bryst, vil de dyr, der voksede op på hans steppe, vende ham ryggen.«
“There he is! Show him your body. Let him enjoy your beauty. Don't hold back. Take his breath away. As soon as he sees you, he'll come to you. Loosen your cloak so he can take you in his arms. Show him what a woman can do. Once he has held you to his chest, the animals raised on his steppe will turn away from him.”

Kvinden blottede sine bryster og lod ham nyde hendes skønhed. Hun holdt sig ikke tilbage. Hun tog pusten fra ham og løsnede sin kappe. Han tog hende i sin favn, og hun viste ham, hvad en kvinde kan, mens han holdt hende ind til sit bryst. Seks dage og syv nætter lå Enkidu med hende.
The woman bared her breasts and let him enjoy her beauty. She did not hold back. She took his breath away and loosened her cloak. He took her in his arms, and she showed him what a woman can do as he held her to his chest. For six days and seven nights Enkidu lay with her.

Da hans lyst var stillet, vendte han sig mod dyrene. Så snart gazellerne så ham, sprang de væk. Steppens dyr flygtede fra Enkidu! Hans krop var forandret. Da dyrene flygtede, svigtede knæene ham. Han løb langsommere end før. Men nu var han blevet voksen og havde fået større indsigt. Han satte sig igen ved kvindens fødder. Hun så ind i hans ansigt. Han lyttede til hendes ord.
When his desire was satisfied, he turned towards the animals. The moment the gazelles saw him they bounded away. The animals of the steppe fled from Enkidu! His body had changed. As the animals fled, his knees failed him. He ran more slowly than before. Yet he had come to maturity and gained understanding. He sat down again at the woman's feet. She looked into his face. He listened to her words.

»Du er smuk, Enkidu. Du er som en gud. Hvorfor strejfer du om på steppen med dyrene? Kom, jeg fører dig til Uruk bag de høje mure, til det hellige tempel, hvor Anu og Ishtar bor. Dér hersker Gilgamesh. Som en urokse er han stærkere end de andre mænd.« Hendes ord passede ham godt, for han mærkede, at han længtes efter en ven.
“You are beautiful, Enkidu. You are like a god. Why roam the steppe with the animals? Come, I'll lead you to high-walled Uruk, to the holy temple where Anu and Ishtar live. Gilgamesh rules there. He outmatches the other men like a wild bull.” Her words pleased him, for he felt a longing for a friend.

»Kom så, tag mig med til det hellige tempel, hvor Anu og Ishtar bor, og hvor Gilgamesh er stærkere end andre mænd, stærk som en urokse. Jeg vil udfordre ham! Jeg vil råbe gennem Uruk: Jeg er også stærk! Jeg selv vil ændre skæbnen. Den, der er født på steppen, har flest kræfter. […] vil glæde dig […] hvad det end er, vil jeg vide det.«
“Come then, take me to the holy temple where Anu and Ishtar live, where Gilgamesh outmatches other men like a wild bull. I'll challenge him! I'll shout through Uruk: I too am strong! I myself will change what fate has set. The man born on the steppe has the greatest strength. […] will please you […] whatever it is, I want to know.”

»Kom til Uruk, Enkidu, byen bag de høje mure. Der trækker folk i festtøjet, og hver dag er en fest. Eunukpræster slår bækkenerne sammen, danserinder […] ler og slår sig løs og holder de fornemme mænd ude af sengen [?]. Du skal smage livets glæde, Enkidu. Jeg vil vise dig en lykkelig mand, Gilgamesh. Se hans ansigt! Se, hvor smuk han er! Hele hans krop er fuld af kraft. Han er stærkere end dig og aldrig i ro, hverken dag eller nat. Dæmp dit overmod, Enkidu. Shamash elsker Gilgamesh, og Anu, Enlil og Ea har givet ham rigeligt med visdom.
“Come to high-walled Uruk, Enkidu. The people put on their finest clothes and every day is a feast. Eunuch priests clash cymbals, dancing girls […] laugh and play and keep the noblemen from their beds [?]. You'll taste the joy of living, Enkidu. I'll show you a happy man, Gilgamesh. Look at his face! Look how handsome he is! His whole body is full of power. He is stronger than you and never rests, day or night. Put aside your boasting, Enkidu. Shamash loves Gilgamesh, and Anu, Enlil and Ea have given him wisdom in abundance.

Allerede før du kom fra dine bjerge, så Gilgamesh dig i en drøm i Uruk. Han gik til sin mor for at fortælle hende drømmen. ‘Mor, i nat så jeg himlens stjerner. Noget, der lignede Anu selv, faldt ned på mine skuldre. Jeg prøvede at løfte det, men det var mig for stærkt. Jeg fik grebet løs, men kunne ikke ryste det af mig. Uruks folk stod rundt om det, håndværkerne trængtes bag det, krigerne flokkedes om det. Mine egne kammerater kyssede dets fødder. Selv tog jeg det ind til brystet, som var det en kvinde. Så lagde jeg det ved dine fødder, for at du skulle regne det for min lige.’
“Even before you came down from your mountains, Gilgamesh saw you in a dream in Uruk. He went to tell his mother the dream. ‘Mother, last night I saw the stars of heaven. Something that resembled Anu himself fell on my shoulders. I tried to lift it but it was too strong for me. I loosened its hold but couldn't shake it off. The people of Uruk stood around it, craftspeople pressed in behind it, warriors crowded round it. My own companions kissed its feet. I held it to my chest as I would a woman. Then I laid it at your feet for you to count it my equal.’

Hun, der kender al visdom, svarede sin herre. Hun, der kender al visdom, svarede Gilgamesh: ‘Himlens stjerner er dine kammerater. Det, der lignede Anu og faldt ned på dine skuldre, det, du prøvede at løfte, men som var dig for stærkt, det, du ikke kunne ryste af dig, selv da du fik grebet løs – det lagde du ved mine fødder, for at jeg skulle regne det for din lige. Du tog det ind til brystet, som var det en kvinde. Det er en modig mand, en ven, der står ved sin kammerat. Ingen i hele landet er stærkere. Han er så stærk, at han kunne være Anus dobbeltgænger. At du tog ham ind til brystet som en kvinde, betyder, at han aldrig vil forlade dig. Det er, hvad din drøm betyder.’
“She who knows all wisdom answered her lord. She who knows all wisdom answered Gilgamesh. ‘The stars of heaven are your companions. The thing that resembled Anu and fell on your shoulders, the thing you tried to lift that was too strong for you, the thing you could not shake off even after you loosened its hold – you laid it at my feet for me to count it your equal. You held it to your chest as you would a woman. It is a man of courage, a friend who stands by a comrade. No one in all the land is stronger. He is so strong he might be Anu's double. Your holding him to your chest as you would a woman means he will never leave you. That is the meaning of your dream.’

Han sagde igen til sin mor: ‘Mor, jeg havde en drøm til. En økse kom susende ind i Uruk bag de høje mure. Folk samledes om den. Uruks folk stod rundt om den. De trængtes foran den, håndværkerne flokkedes bag den. Jeg lagde den ved dine fødder. Jeg holdt den ind til mig som en kvinde, for at du skulle regne den for min lige.’
“He spoke to his mother again. ‘Mother, I had another dream. An axe came hurtling into high-walled Uruk. People gathered round it. The people of Uruk stood around it. They crowded in front of it, craftspeople pressed in behind it. I laid it at your feet. I held it close as I would a woman, for you to count it my equal.’

Hun, der kender al visdom, svarede sin søn. Hun, der kender al visdom, svarede Gilgamesh: ‘Den økse, du så, er en mand. Du holdt ham ind til brystet som en kvinde, for at jeg skulle regne ham for din lige. Det er en modig mand, en ven, der står ved sin kammerat. Ingen i hele landet er stærkere. Han er så stærk, at han kunne være Anus dobbeltgænger.’ Gilgamesh sagde til sin mor: ‘Selv hvis jeg kommer i stor fare [?], skal jeg få en ven [?] […]’.« [Slutningen er beskadiget. En ældre babylonsk tavle overlapper her beretningen.]
“She who knows all wisdom answered her son. She who knows all wisdom answered Gilgamesh. ‘The axe you saw is a man. You held him to your chest as you would a woman, for me to count him your equal. He is a man of courage, a friend who stands by a comrade. No one in all the land is stronger. He is so strong he might be Anu's double.’ Gilgamesh said to his mother, ‘Even if great danger comes [?], I will have a friend [?] […].’” [The ending is damaged. An older Babylonian tablet overlaps the account here.]
